  "teacherNotes": [
    {
      "id": "ket-trainer-1:test-1:training:reading_writing:part-1:teacher-overview",
      "role": "teacher_notes",
      "sectionId": "reading_writing",
      "partNumber": 1,
      "groupIds": [
        "ket-trainer-1:test-1:training:reading_writing:part-1:complete-training"
      ],
      "kind": "teaching_guidance",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "text",
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Task type: three-option multiple choice – understanding notices, emails and messages – six items\nTopic focus: language of notices\nTraining\nTell students that they will read six texts (notices, emails or messages) in Part 1."
        }
      ],
      "provenance": {
        "origin": "source",
        "sources": [
          {
            "sourceId": "ket-trainer-1",
            "pdfPage": 165,
            "printedPage": "164",
            "role": "teacher_notes"
          }
        ],
        "authorship": "ai_assisted",
        "review": {
          "status": "pending"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ket-trainer-1:test-1:training:reading_writing:part-1:teacher-exercise-1",
      "role": "teacher_notes",
      "sectionId": "reading_writing",
      "partNumber": 1,
      "groupIds": [
        "ket-trainer-1:test-1:training:reading_writing:part-1:complete-training"
      ],
      "kind": "teaching_guidance",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "text",
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "1 In this exercise, students decide whether each pair of words/phrases are similar or different in meaning. Tell students that the notices, emails and messages in Part 1 often use similar words. Ask them to write S (similar) or D (different) next to each pair. Check the answers as a class.\nElicit or give students a few more pairs that are similar or different, e.g. money / cash, lesson / class, football / ball game, safe / dangerous."
        }
      ],
      "provenance": {
        "origin": "source",
        "sources": [
          {
            "sourceId": "ket-trainer-1",
            "pdfPage": 165,
            "printedPage": "164",
            "role": "teacher_notes"
          }
        ],
        "authorship": "ai_assisted",
        "review": {
          "status": "pending"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ket-trainer-1:test-1:training:reading_writing:part-1:teacher-exercise-2",
      "role": "teacher_notes",
      "sectionId": "reading_writing",
      "partNumber": 1,
      "groupIds": [
        "ket-trainer-1:test-1:training:reading_writing:part-1:complete-training"
      ],
      "kind": "teaching_guidance",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "text",
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "2 This exercise uses some of the words from Exercise 1 in context. Ask students what sort of text they think each sentence comes from (Example email, 1 notice, 2 message or email, 3 notice, 4 notice or email, 5 notice or email).\nStudents then complete the task, choosing a word from Exercise 1 for each gap.\nExtension\nIn pairs, students make more sentences using some of the remaining words from Exercise 1. Tell students to make two sentences for pairs of words with similar meanings, e.g. He drives to work. / He goes by car."
        }
      ],
      "provenance": {
        "origin": "source",
        "sources": [
          {
            "sourceId": "ket-trainer-1",
            "pdfPage": 165,
            "printedPage": "164",
            "role": "teacher_notes"
          }
        ],
        "authorship": "ai_assisted",
        "review": {
          "status": "pending"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ket-trainer-1:test-1:training:reading_writing:part-1:teacher-exercise-3",
      "role": "teacher_notes",
      "sectionId": "reading_writing",
      "partNumber": 1,
      "groupIds": [
        "ket-trainer-1:test-1:training:reading_writing:part-1:complete-training"
      ],
      "kind": "teaching_guidance",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "text",
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "3 Revise the words children, teenagers and adults. Elicit the age ranges for each word (0~12, 13~19, 18+). Explain that the notices all say that only one age group can do the activity. Students write the two age groups that cannot do it."
        }
      ],
      "provenance": {
        "origin": "source",
        "sources": [
          {
            "sourceId": "ket-trainer-1",
            "pdfPage": 165,
            "printedPage": "164",
            "role": "teacher_notes"
          }
        ],
        "authorship": "ai_assisted",
        "review": {
          "status": "pending"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ket-trainer-1:test-1:training:reading_writing:part-1:teacher-exercise-4",
      "role": "teacher_notes",
      "sectionId": "reading_writing",
      "partNumber": 1,
      "groupIds": [
        "ket-trainer-1:test-1:training:reading_writing:part-1:complete-training"
      ],
      "kind": "teaching_guidance",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "text",
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "4 Before doing this exercise, revise the modals which appear in the box: can, can’t, must, need, should and will. Remind students that they are followed by infinitive without to. Put some examples on the board as well as some common errors, e.g. I must do my homework. [Crossed-out source errors: I must doing my homework. I must to do my homework.]\nIn this exercise, students complete sentences which explain the meaning of the notice by adding the correct modals."
        }
      ],
